A||||9||Lisa 86 2m4||stereo question||||1||
Z||000000||Lisa 86 2m4||01-28-2001||11:33 AM||||Hi all!<BR>I've been looking at getting a new radio/cassette maybe cd player.  My question is as I read the specs, most of them say for 12v negative ground systems.  I figured, whenI put in my keyless entry that I have 12v positive.  What do I need to look for?  And what is DIN? And what DIN should I look for?  And if DIN is the same, will they fit into the pre-existing space? <BR>THanks for your help!<BR>Lisa 86 2M4 Sport Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||01-28-2001||11:43 AM||||the only cars with positive ground were a few english and russan cars with Lucas Girling electronis. all GM cars are negative ground.. if you get a DIN radio you'll need the adapter plate.. if you get a DIN&1/2 it'll fit right in a double DIN won't.. DIn means (Deutch I something N somthing) just a standard measuring size.  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com" TARGET=_blank>www.crutchfield.com</A>    <A HREF="http://www.tweeter.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tweeter.com</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||01-28-2001||11:48 AM||||Well, unless your Fiero is built on an old VW Beetle pan, you have a 12v negative ground system.  It would be highly unusual that someone would modify your electrical system to a positive ground.<P>As far as DIN goes, that stand for (in German) German industry standards.  DIN can be applied to many things. When referring to stereos, it refers to the size of the opening in the dash. GM cars use a "DIN and a half" format.  You can use a standard DIN stereo with an adapter kit, but it will stick out about a half inch.  You can also use a GM/Chrysler format deck like the Pioneer DEH-47/77 dh.  Jensen and Alpine make similar models. <P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||Oreif||01-28-2001||11:51 AM||||The GM size is DIN and 1/2. There are a few companies that make a radio that will bolt in without an adapter. Pioneer, JVC, Clarion to name a few. If you get a DIN size radio, there are adapters. DIN is what most aftermarket radios are. If you want to get real creative some companies make equalizers that are half DIN size. This means you can fit the stereo and the equalizer in the same location as the stock radio. I would recommend the Pioneer stereo's but that is my opinion. Note on DIN adapter. There are 2 kinds for the Fiero. One sticks out and the other is flat. To use the flat one you need to remove the brace at the back of the radio area.<P>This link sells the same stuff as Crutchfield only cheaper.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/</A> <P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||01-28-2001||01:23 PM||||mine sticks out. i can't get my hand in there to get that bracket, and i've got relatively small hands.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||01-28-2001||01:29 PM||||I used a long flat blade screwdriver and lined it up on the rivot. Then used a hammer to "chisel" it off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JasonC||01-28-2001||04:01 PM||||Depending on which adapter you get will some stick out and some are flush. Don't get the "universal kit" <I>it looks cheesy installed</I>.<P>The flush type ones are not out on the shelves for sale. Just go ask the installers for an adapter. Don't buy the 9 - 15 kit, just get the one that fits from the installers. It will be flush and will look a whole lot better.<P>This is in my Grand Prix:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.getpaid2surf.net/97GPGT/images/radio.jpg"> <BR>I bought the adapter plate at BestBuy, it was $12.99.<P>------------------<BR>Jason Carmichael<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net2/smile-r.gif"> <A HREF="http://www.getpaid2surf.net/97GPGT" TARGET=_blank>My GP GT on the net.</A> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net2/smile-l.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Lisa 86 2m4||01-28-2001||04:50 PM||||Thank you ALL for answering!!! You have been a huge help!!! Now, I'm wondering if maybe my trunk release for the keyless entry doesn't work is because I was trying to hook it up as a positive. Hmmmm.<BR>Thanks again!! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Lisa 86 2m4||01-28-2001||09:41 PM||||Thought I would throw this question up here, too.  In looking through the JC Whitney catalog, they have a deluxe overhead speaker system- I've seen them in the back ceilings of SUV's.  Anyone have any idea if they would work on the ceiling of the Fiero behind the seats? Not that I'm going to do it, but it could be cool.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||01-28-2001||11:18 PM||||it's only one speaker, but i have one overhead as a pod that is part of my third brake light. 4" that is angled back toward my back window. it gets a mono signal from an in dash eq. the rear outs are mixed with a 2 into 1 rca plug. still need to work out some delay to it.||||reg||1
